WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.428
Authentication verifies who or what is
making a request.

2
00:00:06.508 --> 00:00:13.547
FastAPI applications may use bearer
tokens, API keys, OAuth flows,

3
00:00:13.627 --> 00:00:18.937
OpenID Connect concepts, mTLS, browser
sessions,

4
00:00:19.017 --> 00:00:26.057
or service-to-service credentials
depending on the environment.

5
00:00:26.137 --> 00:00:30.582
These patterns have different tradeoffs,

6
00:00:30.662 --> 00:00:34.491
but the stable principle is the same:

7
00:00:34.571 --> 00:00:39.511
identify the caller through a trusted
process,

8
00:00:39.591 --> 00:00:47.124
then make sure the result is carried into
the path operation in a clear and

9
00:00:47.204 --> 00:00:48.933
reviewable way.

10
00:00:49.013 --> 00:00:56.423
Signed token validation should check more
than whether a token exists.

11
00:00:56.503 --> 00:01:03.789
At a high level, validation may include
signature integrity, issuer,

12
00:01:03.869 --> 00:01:09.673
audience, expiration, not-before time,
intended use,

13
00:01:09.753 --> 00:01:17.163
and the application's trust relationship
with the identity provider.

14
00:01:17.243 --> 00:01:24.529
A token from the wrong issuer, intended
for another service, expired,

15
00:01:24.609 --> 00:01:32.513
or missing required context should not be
treated as a valid grant of access.

16
00:01:32.593 --> 00:01:36.421
Presence is not proof of permission.

17
00:01:36.501 --> 00:01:43.911
Browser sessions and API tokens solve
related but different problems.

18
00:01:43.991 --> 00:01:52.512
Browser clients may involve cookies,
same-site behavior, CSRF considerations,

19
00:01:52.592 --> 00:01:54.939
and user interaction.

20
00:01:55.019 --> 00:02:02.675
Machine clients may use tokens or keys
with service identity and narrower

21
00:02:02.755 --> 00:02:04.237
permissions.

22
00:02:04.317 --> 00:02:11.480
MFA and step-up authentication can be
useful for high-risk actions,

23
00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:20.205
but they must be integrated into the
actual authorization flow rather than treated

24
00:02:20.285 --> 00:02:22.878
as a label on the account.

25
00:02:22.958 --> 00:02:28.762
Authentication success should not imply
broad access.

26
00:02:28.842 --> 00:02:36.870
A valid user token may still lack
permission for an administrative endpoint.

27
00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:42.754
A valid service token may be limited to
one integration.

28
00:02:42.834 --> 00:02:51.602
A valid browser session may still need
CSRF protection for a state-changing action.

29
00:02:51.682 --> 00:02:59.462
FastAPI security is stronger when
identity, token validity, user intent,

30
00:02:59.542 --> 00:03:07.940
and authorization are treated as separate
checks that happen in the right order.
